# 2. Floating-Point Data Types

| Data Type     |  Typical Size |     Approx. Precision | Format Specifier |
| ------------- | ------------: | --------------------: | ---------------- |
| `float`       |       4 bytes |   ~6–7 decimal digits | `%f`             |
| `double`      |       8 bytes | ~15–16 decimal digits | `%f`             |
| `long double` | 8/12/16 bytes |    Platform dependent | `%Lf`            |

### Example

```cpp
float price = 99.50f;
double salary = 75000.4567;

printf("%f\n", price);
printf("%f\n", salary);
```

For decimal places:

```cpp
printf("%.2f", price);
```

Output:

```text
99.50
```

---

# 3. Character Data Type

| Data Type       | Typical Size | Range / Purpose           | Format Specifier |
| --------------- | -----------: | ------------------------- | ---------------- |
| `char`          |       1 byte | Character / small integer | `%c`             |
| `signed char`   |       1 byte | -128 to 127               | `%hhd`           |
| `unsigned char` |       1 byte | 0 to 255                  | `%hhu`           |

Example:

```cpp
char grade = 'A';

printf("%c", grade);
```

Output:

```text
A
```

A `char` can also store an integer value:

```cpp
char ch = 65;

printf("%c\n", ch);
printf("%d\n", ch);
```

Output:

```text
A
65
```

---

# 4. Boolean Data Type

C++ provides:

```cpp
bool
```

It has two possible values:

```cpp
true
false
```

Example:

```cpp
bool isPassed = true;
```

Using `cout`:

```cpp
cout << isPassed;
```

Output:

```text
1
```

To display `true` / `false`:

```cpp
cout << boolalpha << isPassed;
```

Output:

```text
true
```

For `printf`, there is no dedicated `%bool` format specifier.

You can use:

```cpp
printf("%d", isPassed);
```

---

# 5. `void`

`void` means **no value / no return value**.

Example:

```cpp
void display() {
    cout << "Hello";
}
```

A `void` function does not return a value.

There is no `printf` format specifier for `void`.

